* START-HISTORY:
* 12 Sep 26 dm  New.  Plan section M2, the migration half of the lower-case
*               conversion.  No port equivalent: NTFS matches names regardless
*               of case, so the port could rename new installs and leave old
*               accounts alone.  On ext4 LIST and list are two records.
* 12 Sep 26 dm  $internal: select list 11 is an internal list, and without
*               the flag the select faulted (verify-voccase, install 4288380).
* 12 Sep 26 dm  Names CREATEA writes (not shipped) migrate too, once renamed:
*               $savedlists, the first M3 rename.
* 12 Sep 26 dm  $hold joins created.ids (the second M3 rename).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !voccase(voc.f, newvoc.f, template.f, moved, collided)
*
*    voc.f       the account VOC, open
*    newvoc.f    SDSYS NEWVOC, open
*    template.f  SDSYS VOC_TEMPLATE, open (may be the same file as newvoc.f)
*    moved       returned: the lower-case ids records were moved to, @fm list
*    collided    returned: the ids left alone because BOTH spellings exist
*
*    For every record in voc.f whose id contains an upper-case letter, and
*    whose LOWER-CASE id is a record SD ships in NEWVOC or VOC_TEMPLATE, the
*    record is moved to the lower-case id, content unchanged.  A [locked]
*    marker moves with it, so the update that runs afterwards honours it under
*    the new name exactly as it did under the old one.
*
*    ONLY NAMES SD SHIPS.  A record whose lower-case id SD does not ship is the
*    account's own - a user's paragraph, file or verb - and is not touched:
*    the owner's ruling of 12 Sep 2026 puts user data outside section M.  The
*    same test also leaves a SYSTEM name alone until SD actually ships it in
*    lower case, so this routine is inert on a tree that has not been renamed
*    and each category starts migrating the release it is renamed in.
*
*    ***BOTH SPELLINGS PRESENT IS REFUSED, NOT GUESSED*** (plan M2).  If the
*    lower-case id already exists and the two records DIFFER, neither is
*    touched and the upper-case id is returned in collided for the caller to
*    name.  A fallback that silently picked one would eventually pick the
*    empty one.  If the two are IDENTICAL there is no choice to make, so the
*    upper-case one is deleted and counted as moved - which is also how a run
*    interrupted between the write and the delete below finishes next time.
*
*    WRITE THEN DELETE, never the reverse: interrupted between the two, the
*    account has both spellings of one record (recovered by the identical rule
*    above), never neither.
*
*    Uses select list 11 and reads it to the end before doing anything else,
*    so the caller must not be holding list 11 when it calls.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

* $INTERNAL IS LOAD BEARING, NOT HABIT.  Lists 11 and 12 are refused to any
* program without HDR_INTERNAL (sd.h:43-48, "Select list number out of range")
* - measured 12 Sep 2026, verify-voccase on install 4288380, where this ran
* without it and faulted at its select.  The flag is the running program's,
* so it holds when an ordinary user's sign-in reaches this through update.voc,
* and an internal list is also the one that cannot clobber the user's own.
$internal
subroutine voccase(voc.f, newvoc.f, template.f, moved, collided)
$catalogue !voccase

   moved = ''
   collided = ''

*  12 Sep 26 dm - THE SYSTEM NAMES SD DOES NOT SHIP BUT CREATES.  CREATEA writes
*  $COMMAND.STACK, $HOLD, $SAVEDLISTS and BP into a new account's VOC itself
*  (CREATEA create.dir.file and the write above it); only $HOLD is also in
*  VOC_TEMPLATE.  Those are system names, so they migrate too - but ONLY ONCE
*  RENAMED: an id listed here before CREATEA and the code use it would be moved
*  to a name nothing opens, and $HOLD would be copied straight back from
*  VOC_TEMPLATE beside it.  ***KEEP THIS EQUAL TO THE LOWER-CASE ids CREATEA
*  WRITES*** - verify-voccase.py compares the two in the installed source.
*  An @VM list searched with <1,1>, the form DELETEF:74/:239 uses and has been
*  seen working ("Cannot delete system file VOC").
   created.ids = '$savedlists':@vm:'$hold'

*  Collect first, act second: the loop below writes and deletes records in
*  the very file being selected.
   ids = ''
   select voc.f to 11
   loop
      readnext id from 11 else exit
      if downcase(id) # id then ids<-1> = id
   repeat

   n = dcount(ids, @fm)
   for i = 1 to n
      id = ids<i>
      lid = downcase(id)

      shipped = @false
      locate lid in created.ids<1,1> setting pos then shipped = @true
      if not(shipped) then
         read x from newvoc.f, lid then shipped = @true
      end
      if not(shipped) then
         read x from template.f, lid then shipped = @true
      end
      if not(shipped) then continue

      readu rec from voc.f, id else
         release voc.f, id              ;* gone since the select
         continue
      end

      read lrec from voc.f, lid then
         if lrec = rec then
            delete voc.f, id            ;* identical: nothing to choose
            moved<-1> = lid
         end else
            release voc.f, id
            collided<-1> = id
         end
         continue
      end

      write rec to voc.f, lid
      delete voc.f, id
      moved<-1> = lid
   next i

   return
end

* END-CODE
